@* "What is a CNN?" -- a plain-language explainer for the risk model (risk_model.py:
   5-fold ConvNeXt-Base CNN embeddings + ABCD features + age/sex/body site into CatBoost,
   fold scores averaged). Keep this in step with that file if the model changes. *@

<InfoPopover Title="What is a CNN?" Label="@Label">
    <p>
        A <strong>CNN</strong> (convolutional neural network) is a kind of AI built for pictures.
        It looks at a photo in small overlapping patches. The first layers pick up simple things
        like edges and changes in colour; later layers combine those into bigger patterns, such
        as the shape of a border, texture, and how colours mix.
    </p>
    <p>
        It isn't given rules like "irregular border means concern". It learns which patterns matter
        from examples. The CNN here (ConvNeXt) was trained on dermatology photos from the ISIC skin
        image archive, each labelled as melanoma or not.
    </p>
    <p>
        <strong>How LA Spot uses it:</strong> the CNN turns your photo into a summary of the patterns
        it sees. A second model then combines that summary with the ABCD measurements and, if you've
        given them, your age, sex and the spot's body location. Five versions of the model, each
        trained on a different slice of the data, score the photo, and you see their average.
    </p>
    <p>
        <strong>Its limits:</strong> it can only recognise patterns like the ones in its training photos.
        Blur, poor lighting, and skin tones that were less common in that data can make it less
        reliable. The score is a screening aid, not a diagnosis.
    </p>
</InfoPopover>

@code {
    /// <summary>Text beside the "i"; null shows the icon alone.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string? Label { get; set; } = "What is a CNN?";
}
